Below is an iterative gene set enrichment (iGEA) network computed from a gene list. Iterative means that for each gene set collection, the top enriched gene set is found, saved and its genes are removed from the gene list; the remaining genes are then tested again. Thus each gene appears at most once per collection but can be linked to terms from different collections. The network of genes and gene sets (= terms) is given in DOT format, preceded by its statistics and communities.

**STATISTICAL CONTEXT:**
The input contained 426 validated genes. iGEA selected 80 terms; 70 passed the term-level filter (q-value < 0.1, an empirical false discovery rate estimated from 10,000 random gene lists of the same size, background and settings) and form this network. A random gene list of this size selects about 22.0 terms by chance.

Network-level test: the largest connected cluster of this network compared with the networks built the same way (same filter) from the 10,000 random gene lists. Empirical p = (r + 1) / (10,000 + 1), where r is the number of random lists with a value at least as large. The metrics describe the same cluster and are not independent tests.

| Metric | This network | Random mean | Empirical p |
|---|---|---|---|
| Nodes (genes + terms) in the largest connected cluster | 343 | 6.2 | p < 0.0001 (no random list reached this value) |
| Genes in the largest cluster | 273 | 5.34 | p < 0.0001 (no random list reached this value) |
| Terms in the largest cluster | 70 | 0.856 | p < 0.0001 (no random list reached this value) |
| Edges in the largest cluster | 691 | 6.01 | p < 0.0001 (no random list reached this value) |
| Average edges per gene | 2.531 | 0.656 | p = 0.0029 |

How to use the statistics: the network is not distinguishable from what random gene lists produce if the empirical p for the number of nodes in the largest cluster is 0.05 or higher. In that case, say so plainly, keep the rest short and tentative, and do not propose an experimental context. Do not use p-values, q-values or iteration numbers to rank biological importance.

**COMMUNITIES:**
Terms were grouped into communities with Leiden (igraph, modularity) on the term network, in which two terms are linked when they share genes (modularity 0.3681; the same partition was found with 91% of 100 random seeds). Communities are algorithmic groupings, not biological labels; the cluster blocks in the DOT list each community's terms and genes. Gene degree is the number of terms a gene connects to; in iGEA this equals the number of collections that place the gene in the network. Bridge genes connect terms in different communities.

| Community | Terms | Genes | Lead term (most genes) | Top hub genes (degree) | Bridge genes |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| C1 | 13 | 109 | REACTOME: NEUTROPHIL DEGRANULATION | TCIRG1 (6), ATP6V0A1 (5), ATP6V0D1 (5), CD36 (5), GBA1 (5) | 45 |
| C2 | 18 | 82 | GOBP: CYTOKINE MEDIATED SIGNALING PATHWAY | CASP1 (6), APAF1 (5), CD4 (5), CSF3R (5), NOD2 (5) | 22 |
| C3 | 15 | 77 | GOBP: PHAGOCYTOSIS | MAPK1 (8), HCK (7), PAK1 (7), PRKCD (7), NCF1 (6) | 26 |
| C4 | 13 | 64 | GOBP: AMIDE METABOLIC PROCESS | HK1 (4), NAGK (4), NPL (4), PPCDC (4), KDELR1 (3) | 3 |
| C5 | 7 | 52 | GOCC: CELL LEADING EDGE | ACTR3 (5), CYFIP1 (4), CAPG (3), CAPZB (3), CORO1B (3) | 6 |
| C6 | 4 | 6 | GOBP: ALCOHOL CATABOLIC PROCESS | SULT1A1 (4), SULT1A3 (4), SULT1A4 (4), CHST15 (1), GK (1) | 0 |

